    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Jainism, taught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the same age as the Buddha, holds that every living being has a soul and that release from rebirth comes through non-violence, truth and the renunciation of possessions.</li><li>Its monks and nuns live by five great vows of extreme strictness, while its lay followers, many of them merchants and bankers, became one of the most prosperous and influential communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li><li>Divided since about the 1st century CE into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> and Svetambara traditions, it built temples of rare beauty and influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s ideal of non-violence.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The colossal statue of Bahubali at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, carved from a single block of granite around 983, stands about 17 metres high and is anointed with milk, saffron and flowers every twelve years.</p></aside>
    </section>

